  "fields": {
    "title": "WYSIWYG",
    "textContent": {
      "nodeType": "document",
      "data": {},
      "content": [
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Description",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "WYSIWYG, short for ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "»what you see ist what you get« ",
              "marks": [
                {
                  "type": "italic"
                }
              ],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "means that a document is displayed on the screen while editing exactly as it looks when it is output via another device, e.g. a printer. Current examples of WYSIWYG programmes are Adobe InDesign or Microsoft Word. ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Website builders and ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "7gVQobWbxFti05whwYInu5",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "no-code editors",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " are sometimes also referred to as WYSIWYG applications, but strictly speaking this is not correct. Since the output of an HTML page depends strongly on the browser used and its default settings as well as the display device, it can vary greatly from viewer to viewer. ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Background",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "The term emerged during the late 1970s at the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center when the first WYSIWYG editor, Bravo, was developed on Alto by Charles Simonyi. After that it was used in the early 1980s in connection with computer typesetting systems (desktop publishing) and word processors. The first commercially available WYSIWYG device was the ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Satz-Gestaltungs-Terminal (SGT)",
              "marks": [
                {
                  "type": "italic"
                }
              ],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": ", developed by the Austrian company Grafotron.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "In the 1990s, the term WYSIWYG was blurred by numerous programmes in which the output document only looked similar, but not identical to what it looked like during editing. ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
